            "rubricHtml": "<p>Questions 31–40</p>",
            "selectCount": 1,
            "bodyHtml": "<p>Complete the notes below. Write <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> for each answer.</p><div><div>Dormancy</div><p>Organisms enter a state of dormancy, or inactivity, to:</p><ul><li>save available {{31}}</li><li>reduce demands on their environment</li></ul><p>Value of dormancy — organisms which become dormant:</p><ul><li>can live in conditions which are environmentally {{32}}</li><li>can live in a wider range of places</li><li>have less {{33}} from other animals</li></ul><p>Causes of dormancy</p><ul><li>changes in temperature and {{34}}</li><li>availability of food, water, oxygen</li></ul><p>Dormancy in insects</p><ul><li>there may be a {{35}} in their metabolism (diapause)</li><li>diapause occurs during extreme conditions, or as part of the life cycle</li></ul><p>The African lungfish</p><ul><li>it buries itself in the {{36}} and creates a slimy cocoon</li><li>two dangers: {{37}} and desiccation</li></ul><p>Snails</p><ul><li>some close their shells with a membrane during dormancy</li><li>light-coloured shells reflect sunlight and limit {{38}}</li></ul><p>Hibernation</p><ul><li>only warm-blooded animals hibernate</li></ul><p>The Arctic ground squirrel</p><ul><li>it hibernates in an insulated, underground {{39}}</li><li>it loses 40% of its weight during hibernation</li><li>when it wakes up, the first part of the body to heat up is the {{40}}</li></ul></div>",
            "maxWords": 2
          }
        ]
